WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.210
It doesn't get much better than reruns of
The Office, and sometimes you notice things

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:05.160
you never caught before.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.840
From creepy clown paintings to Call of Duty
​screen names, here are some of the best

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.320
Easter eggs to look out for the next time
you settle in to binge watch America's favorite

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.500
workplace in action.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.680
Even the most casual viewers of The Office
know that the American series didn't begin

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:18.740
with an original idea.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.500
It's a remake inspired by a British show by
the same name.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.740
This original series ran from 2001 to 2003
and focused on the fictional paper company

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.860
Wernham Hogg in the British town of Slough.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:32.600
The American show takes place in Scranton,
Pennsylvania, but it still has a connection

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:34.270
to its British cousin's hometown.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:38.300
In the season 5 episode "Blood Drive," Michael
pulls together a depressing little Valentines

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.190
Day shindig for all of his single coworkers,
harboring secret hopes that he will be able

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:44.360
to reconnect with a woman he met earlier in
the day.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.410
As he shows off the flyer he's created for
the event, the camera zooms in, revealing

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.760
that the address for Dunder Mifflin is "1725
Slough Ave."

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.400
The address seems to be a nod to the location
of the original Wernham Hogg paper company.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.899
It's not a one-time incident either, in the
last episode of season 6, Dwight refers to

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:00.980
the street by name again.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:02.200
"Cancel it.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.600
I want you to make an offer at 1725 Slough
Avenue.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.600
Make 'em an offer they can't refuse."

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:13.160
The story of Angela and Dwight's secret love
is a long one, starting early on in the show

00:01:13.179 --> 00:01:16.929
and winding through several ups and downs
before they finally tie the knot, Schrute

00:01:16.929 --> 00:01:19.440
style, while standing in their own graves
in the series finale.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.900
Truly, they're the show's perfect match, to
the point where they even seem to have the

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:23.960
same favorite song.

00:01:24.149 --> 00:01:27.600
It's made clear from early on that Angela
is a huge fan of "The Little Drummer Boy."

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.740
Every time a karaoke machine is fired up,
that's the song she looks for.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.700
She even asks Andy if they can use it for
their first dance when they're planning their

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:34.200
wedding.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:38.460
"Is there any way our first dance can be to
my favorite song?

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:40.619
The Little Drummer Boy?"

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.219
With that in mind, it's interesting that,
in the pilot for the show, it's none other

00:01:44.219 --> 00:01:47.499
than the Assistant to the Regional Manager
himself who is shown aggressively humming

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.580
that particular carol as he sets up for work.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.420
"Mmm no gifts for you, parumpumpumpum."

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.120
Does this imply that the relationship between
the two goes back even further than the beginning

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:02.640
of the show?

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.180
Either way, the connection seems too clear
to be an accident.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.220
The whole teapot episode in season 2 is an
iconic moment in the Pam and Jim timeline.

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:14.150
After Michael turns a simple Secret Santa
Christmas party into an impromptu white elephant

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:17.770
gift swap, Jim is frustrated to find that
his romantically-intentioned teapot gift for

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.320
Pam has fallen into the hands of his nemesis,
Dwight.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.739
"I get sinus infections and sinus infections
can be cured by making a tea from green tea

00:02:24.739 --> 00:02:25.819
leaves stems…"

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:26.700
"Okay."

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:27.880
"...and pouring it into your nose like so."

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:33.060
Pam ultimately makes the right move, though,
by choosing to exchange her fancy new iPod

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.040
for Jim's thoughtful (if humble) gift instead.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.700
But that isn't the only time that the gift
appears in the show.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:41.689
Almost two years later, in the season 4 episode
"Branch Wars," Pam meets with the exquisitely

00:02:41.689 --> 00:02:43.060
refined "Finer Things Club."

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.060
As the club enjoys their fancy lunch, the
very same teapot Pam got from her soon-to-be

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.360
beau shows up on the table.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.120
Here's hoping it had been washed thoroughly,
hopefully several times, since Dwight shoved

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:52.980
it up his nose.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.680
While the show focuses pretty tightly on a
small handful of characters, the rest of Dunder

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:00.890
Mifflin's employees usually have their own
lives going on in the background, complete

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:02.310
with their own long-running plots.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:06.379
Take, for instance, Stanley's troubled marriage,
which only gets worse as the series goes on.

00:03:06.379 --> 00:03:10.480
The issue even comes to a head in the season
6 opener, "Gossip," in which Michael leaks

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.920
the secret of Stanley having an affair to
the entire office.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:16.870
While that episode ends with Stanley getting
a small measure of cathartic revenge, it turns

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:20.600
out that the struggling salesman actually
does want to improve his relationships…sort

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:21.100
of.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.420
In the season 7 episode "Ultimatum," Pam encourages
her coworkers to come up with New Year's resolutions.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.920
Some of these are minor items, like Pam attempting
to drink less caffeine, but others get pretty

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:35.560
ridiculous, like Creed trying to do the perfect
cartwheel or Dwight's search for…well, let's

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:36.700
just call it romance.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.320
"My resolution is: meet a loose woman."

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.920
The resolution that has to take the cake,
though, is Stanley's double commitment to,

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.159
quote, "be a better husband and boyfriend."

00:03:49.159 --> 00:03:52.359
Each of those on their own is an admirable
goal, but it might be pretty difficult to

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:53.800
pull off both at the same time.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.400
As the last two seasons of The Office unfold,
Dwight slowly but surely sees his life fall

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.200
into place in virtually every way.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:05.819
He finds love, unwittingly has a son, gets
his black belt, inherits a 1,600 acre beet

00:04:05.819 --> 00:04:09.780
farm, and finally, at long last, becomes the
regional manager of the Scranton Branch of

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:10.520
Dunder Mifflin.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:12.640
It's not just the big stuff, though.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.320
Dwight's life is so perfect at this point
that even his smallest, most obscure wishes

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.410
start to come true.

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:18.820
Case in point: the rocking chair.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:23.130
When Dwight accompanies Michael to a cocktail
party at David Wallace's house way back in

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:27.200
Season 3, he spends time admiring a wooden
rocking chair in Wallace's son's bedroom,

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.940
and declares that he wants one for himself.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:33.160
"Oh, good, you're up.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:34.880
Hey, who makes this chair?"

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:37.690
Cut to Season 9, more than half a decade later.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:41.720
In the episode "The Farm," Dwight is interviewed
as he sits in a gorgeous wooden rocking chair

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.580
that just so happens to look exactly like
the one he was wishing for six seasons earlier.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:50.160
Michael Scott is proud of his possessions,
and nowhere is this made more clear than in

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.260
the season 4 episode "Dinner Party," in which
Michael exhibits his pathetically small plasma

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:55.260
screen television.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:58.680
"I will just stand here and watch television
for hours.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:04:59.520
I love it.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:01.980
I love this TV."

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:05.880
Later in the episode, Jan throws one of Michael's
many Dundee Awards at it, cracking the screen

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.820
and infuriating Michael in the process.

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:11.860
That isn't the last time we see Michael's
beloved and broken miniature TV, though.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:16.110
The wall-mounted wonder made a cameo a year
later, tucked behind another TV, in the fifth

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.760
season episode "Dream Team."

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.220
If that wasn't enough, it shows up yet again
nearly two whole seasons later in the episode

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:25.560
"Garage Sale," sitting plain as day on Michael
and Holly's table.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:29.200
It looks like the much more grounded influence
of Holly may have finally convinced Michael

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.120
to let go of his prized entertainment possession
once and for all.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:36.900
As an interesting little side note, the garage
sale also shows the creepy clown painting

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.520
first seen when Jim buys Pam a house in season
5.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:43.260
Hopefully whoever bought it was able to keep
it out of the hands of the notorious Scranton

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:43.940
art thieves.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.020
"I tried to move it, but he is really nailed
in there.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:49.600
Worried about art theft, I guess.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:51.700
Lots of art thieves in this neighborhood."

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:57.470
In the season 2 episode "The Injury," Michael
Scott wakes up and steps on his George Foreman

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.290
grill, cooking his foot in the process.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.200
While the injury is minor, he ends up milking
it all day long.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.920
When asked why he had a George Foreman grill
by his bedside in the first place, he explains

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:09.840
that every night before he goes to sleep he
lays out six pieces of bacon on a grill in

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:10.340
his bedroom.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:13.800
When he wakes up, he pops on the grill, goes
back to sleep, and then wakes up a second

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.880
time to the delicious smell of sizzling bacon.

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:19.600
"It is delicious, it's good for me, it's the
perfect way to start the day."

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.180
It appears that Michael's morning bacon habits
die hard.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:27.501
The grill shows up yet again two seasons later,
in the season 4 episode "Dinner Party" as

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:30.840
Michael and Jan give Jim and Pam a rather
uncomfortable tour of their bedroom.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:35.020
For all of her quirky, erratic behavior, Jan's
apparently okay with the smell of bacon each

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:37.060
morning…after it's been sitting out all
night.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:41.300
A good portion of season 3 follows Jim as
he tries to get away from his crush on Pam

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:44.560
and attempts to begin a new life at the Dunder
Mifflin branch in Stamford.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:48.880
One episode, "The Coup," follows the Stamford
squad as they practice team-building exercises

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:50.840
by playing Call of Duty against each other.

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:54.110
The group is highly skilled at playing the
video game at this point, but the Scranton

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:56.600
newcomer spectacularly fails to get on the
bandwagon.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.000
"Saboteur!

00:06:58.740 --> 00:06:59.340
Saboteur!"

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:00.120
"Andy, it's not-"

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:01.240
"I'm gonna kill you for real."

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:06.290
In order to give her new coworker a hand,
Karen shows Jim how to navigate out of a corner,

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:07.720
only to shoot him a moment later.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:11.280
That's when we get to see her screen name
for these inter-office deathmatches: "Karen

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:12.120
the Jim Slayer."

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:14.400
Pretty specific, but also pretty appropriate.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:18.580
Michael Scott has been known to bend the truth
from time to time, and that's to be expected

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:22.380
from a guy who was Dunder Mifflin's top salesman
for two years running back in the day.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.580
When it comes to his management skills, though,
the man seems a bit more insecure…and determined

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:27.620
to prove a point.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:31.030
There's the famous World's Best Boss mug that
he bought himself, one of the first things

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:34.770
we see in the show's pilot, but that's not
Michael's only dubious claim to fame.

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:38.500
A plaque holding a certification can frequently
be seen on the wall of his office.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:42.320
Sharp-eyed viewers may note that it's not
a diploma, it's a "Certificate of Authenticity"

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:42.820
for his watch.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:43.640
Even better?

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:47.640
Judging by the slightly misspelled brand name,
it identifies Michael as the proud owner of

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:48.240
a knockoff.

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:51.740
While the writing on the certificate can be
difficult to make out, there's one episode

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:55.390
where it's clearly visible, and it makes for
an often overlooked second layer to a gag.

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:59.870
You can see it in the season 4 finale "Goodbye
Toby," which also happens to be the same episode

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:03.860
in which Michael is forced to give his watch
to Toby as a goodbye present after being an

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.180
awful boss for so many years.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:09.540
"I am going to give Toby...

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:10.040
"

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:11.000
"Your watch?"

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.520
If that's not poetic justice, we don't know
what is.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:20.960
There's a good portion of the series devoted
to the ongoing duel between Assistant Regional

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:21.460
Managers.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:24.360
In one corner of the ring, we have the obvious
candidate Dwight Schrute.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:27.030
"That's why you have an Assistant Regional
Manager."

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:28.020
"Yes, it is.

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:29.900
Assistant to the Regional Manager."

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:30.420
"Same thing."

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:31.680
"No, it's not.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:32.900
It's lower, so."

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:33.800
"It's close."

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:37.660
His endless pursuit to escape being the "Assistant
to the Regional Manager" doesn't quite end

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:41.860
until he leapfrogs the issue entirely by being
made the full-blown manager in season 9, at

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:45.840
which time he appoints himself as his own
assistant to the assistant to the regional

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:46.340
manager.

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:48.140
In the other corner, we have Jim Halpert.

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:52.520
While he does spend some time as "co-manager"
in season 6, Jim actually climbs the corporate

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:55.720
ladder to the Assistant Regional Manager position
much faster than that.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:09:00.720
In the season 3 episode "Diwali", Jim, Karen,
and Andy have to work late and end up drinking

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:01.960
a bit too much while they're at it.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.720
At one point, the camera shows Jim, passed
out on his desk with his name placard in front

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:06.220
of him.

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:08.060
It reads, "Jim Halpert Asst.

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:09.200
Regional Manager."

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:10.820
Not a single "to the" in sight.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:11.860
Seems we have a winner.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:15.640
Most of the time, Creed is the one in the
office who isn't quite what he seems.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:21.070
"I run a small, fake ID company from my car
with a laminating machine that I swiped from

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:21.560
the sheriff's station."

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:26.500
However, in one episode it's none other than
Jim Halpert who seems to have a secret identity.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:30.900
As all truly devoted fans will recall, season
4 kicks off with Meredith Palmer being struck

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.220
by Michael Scott's car.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.540
It's a banner incident in the show that kicks
off a running joke and even makes it into

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:39.480
the montage of incidents referenced in the
"Seasons of Love" parody that the crew sings

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:44.070
to Michael at his very last Dundies ceremony,
right before he exits the show in season 7.

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:48.321
When Meredith returns to work after the accident
in the season 4 episode "Launch Party," she

00:09:48.321 --> 00:09:49.780
asks Jim to sign her cast.

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:53.740
He agrees before realizing that Meredith's
injured pelvis has presented him with a pretty

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:55.400
unusual place sign a cast.

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:59.720
Needless to say, it's a little uncomfortable,
even without Meredith's awkward flirting.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:02.640
So uncomfortable, in fact, that it might just
have broken Jim's character.

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:06.320
As he awkwardly signs his name, fans might
notice that it looks an awful lot like it

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:08.830
says "John Krasinski" rather than "Jim Halpert."

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:12.300
He even dots two i's when he's finished, his
character's name only has one.

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:16.720
The Schrute family is well known for its sense
of tradition, longevity, and attention to

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:17.220
detail.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:20.580
While this can be seen in everything from
the venerable Schrute Farms family home to

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:24.240
Dwight's impeccable work performance, there's
one thing that sums it all up perfectly: the

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:25.380
American flag.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:27.180
Not just any American flag, though.

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:30.320
We're talking about the one flying from the
porch of Schrute Farms.

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:34.240
When some of the group visits Dwight on his
family farm in the episode "Doomsday" in season

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:35.990
8, they pass a flag on their way out.

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:39.560
If you look closely, it's missing some stars,
35, to be precise.

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:45.040
The 15-star flag was used between 1795 and
1818, which also happens to be the period

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:46.700
during which Schrute farms was founded.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:51.120
The accuracy isn't just fun to spot, it's
also appropriately located on the home of

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:54.860
the most likely person in the entire office
who would care about a detail like that in

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:55.860
the first place.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:59.820
The season 5 episode "The Surplus" follows
the office as they struggle to decide how

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:03.260
to spend a budgetary surplus before it reverts
back to corporate.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:07.560
The decision splits the group into two factions:
one in favor of getting new chairs, the other

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:08.680
for getting a new copier.

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:13.190
Critically, Pam and Jim find themselves on
opposite sides of the discussion, making this

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:16.580
one of the few times that the newly engaged
couple are pitted against one another.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:20.720
At one point, Oscar and Jim take Michael out
to lunch in order to schmooze him about the

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:22.110
benefits of getting a new copier.

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:25.620
When they return, Jim brings Pam some tiramisu
from the restaurant.

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:29.370
Pam jokingly takes the gift with a smile,
only to then throw it into the garbage can

00:11:29.370 --> 00:11:30.120
in defiance.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:32.700
Clearly, Pam will not be bought off with fancy
cake.

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:36.600
The back and forth is comical, but it turns
out that the delicious dessert hasn't hit

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:37.790
the end of the line quite yet.

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:42.001
A bit later in the episode, Michael is seen
eating tiramisu as he talks to David Wallace

00:11:42.001 --> 00:11:43.001
on the phone.

00:11:43.001 --> 00:11:46.279
Then, to bring it all home, during a big speech
designed to cover the fact that he's shooting

00:11:46.279 --> 00:11:50.230
for a bonus by returning the surplus to corporate,
Michael reveals where he got his snack from

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:52.060
as he berates the office for being spoiled.

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:56.600
"We are spoiled because we throw out perfectly
good tiramisu because it has a little, tiny

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:57.220
hair on it."

00:11:58.220 --> 00:11:59.350
The most surprising thing?

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:02.240
That's not even the weirdest food choice Michael
makes in this one episode.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:06.140
Every show has its list of slip-ups and continuity
mistakes.

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:09.760
The Office, for instance, will occasionally
show an exterior shot with a suspiciously

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:14.240
Californian palm tree on what's supposed to
be a chilly morning in Scranton, Pennsylvania,

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:17.780
something that's especially noticeable in
Season 3's "Traveling Salesman."

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:21.310
There are a few others that slipped through
the cracks, though, and you can spot one in

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:22.260
season 8's "Pool Party."

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:26.290
In the episode, the staff heads over to Robert
California's house after hours in order to

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:29.330
throw one last party before he sells the home
off to the highest bidder.

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:33.210
While most of the crew spends time taking
advantage of their boss' luxurious indoor

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:37.790
pool, another group is taken on a long, boozy
tour of the mansion led by Mr. California

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:38.280
himself.

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:42.940
As they go along, observing each room and
sucking up to California as he endlessly monologues,

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:46.540
each member of the tour is gifted with their
own bottle of wine, recklessly taken from

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:48.240
the soon-to-be-sold wine cellar.

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:52.250
At one point, as they observe a "king size"
bear rug made from two bears sewn together,

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:56.200
Oscar takes a swig from his bottle, only to
reveal a note on the bottom that reads "Oscar"

00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:57.340
that wasn't there earlier.

00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:01.600
Either the accountant is extremely particular
about labeling his things, or somebody forgot

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:04.380
to remove the prop label before the cameras
started rolling.

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:08.580
The Office is known for having some of the
best cold opens in sitcom history.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:10.720
Michael and Dwight's attempt at making a parkour
video?

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:14.480
Michael's truly incredible, wildly gif-worthy
reaction to Toby's return?

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:18.220
Randall Park's appearance as "Asian Jim" for
one of the most elaborate entries in Jim and

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:19.520
Dwight's ongoing prank war?

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:23.560
They're all classics, but one of the best
cold opens for the show takes place in the

00:13:23.561 --> 00:13:25.330
season 3 episode "Product Recall."

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:29.450
In it, Jim arrives for work decked out in
an outfit that makes him look just like Dwight.

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:33.750
From the dorky haircut to the glasses, short
sleeved shirt, and beeper, the entire ensemble

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:34.540
is on point.

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:35.660
"Bears.

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:36.900
Beets.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:38.400
Battlestar Galactica."

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:42.420
After his initial arrival, Jim explains that
he got the outfit on the cheap, spending just

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:46.200
four bucks on the glasses and pulling the
rest of the pieces together for a mere seven

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:47.040
extra dollars.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:50.580
According to the total that he adds up on
his Dwight-esque calculator watch, the grand

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:53.460
total for the prank was the very affordable
sum of eleven dollars.

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:57.360
It's a memorable joke and one of the highlights
of the series, but what really puts it over

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:00.450
the top is a subtle moment that we're treated
to at the end of the episode.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:04.500
Angela, frustrated by a day of filling in
on customer service calls, rips into Kevin

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:07.410
for failing to add up the numbers on a payroll
form correctly.

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:08.440
The numbers in question?

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:12.830
The same ones added up on the calculator watch
back in the cold open: Four plus seven.

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:17.060
That's a little too specific to be a coincidence,
so we're left with one amazing fact: not only

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:21.370
did Jim pull off an A-plus prank, but he also
managed to get reimbursed for it as a business

00:14:21.370 --> 00:14:21.860
expense.

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:25.340
Hey, if Michael can spend company money hand
over fist for all those big dinners at the

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:28.960
local Chili's, then Jim should be able to
expense costs incurred while boosting office

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:29.460
morale.

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:33.220
The only question left is whether Dwight thought
to file those same forms when he dressed up

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:33.720
as Jim.

